c64-935de2ba2369" :question/math-academy-id "q-76110" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/760ddc657e8372a3860e2553e4f2b713a8b12544586c708e5f2c1922462567a8.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=3$  but this is already excluded.  \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$  and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals. \n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-2,3)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-3,4)$ and $(0,5)$ contain the excluded point $x=3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and  $(-\\infty ,0)$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=-3$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76110/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76110"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" :answer/id #uuid "485ca6f5-2099-5d35-9faf-4669ec15927a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection/7a63746fb154abad8b03f812bbe07834b58e3ebbe52640c382bc0d9cd40b4132" :answer/id #uuid "9a3fffb7-d329-5f49-9031-290aae4aee3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(1,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection/094405769b8442fd1f6f3dc6f2d67d541ddf6f89934b4f55cc1c88a0f3e68dd1" :answer/id #uuid "9e49c6e1-4d55-5bee-a8e3-a4d8b895533f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" :answer/id #uuid "57a0a417-e194-5969-ada8-bbb8dea4d316" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection/d65b3b6c10f909eb18fa4e3b548cec65c464a1fb2acfd1aa6df0726d7bc90150" :answer/id #uuid "f347298c-527a-5d74-a5b1-15b33fe1b22a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "08483342-8e51-5b2f-b963-30d881265e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104881/selection/094405769b8442fd1f6f3dc6f2d67d541ddf6f89934b4f55cc1c88a0f3e68dd1" :answer-field/choices ["q-104881/selection/680ec8692b6fc95fb2227343f14aa1568389f35ada9f43a9c2656345cc979f2a" "q-104881/selection/7a63746fb154abad8b03f812bbe07834b58e3ebbe52640c382bc0d9cd40b4132" "q-104881/selection/094405769b8442fd1f6f3dc6f2d67d541ddf6f89934b4f55cc1c88a0f3e68dd1" "q-104881/selection/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" "q-104881/selection/d65b3b6c10f909eb18fa4e3b548cec65c464a1fb2acfd1aa6df0726d7bc90150"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104881" :question/id #uuid "c2bf1f76-1005-59d7-a147-484f36ea43f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104881" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d6efb9c0bc8d7114c23ad3276303fe6898afcc4e27ad32db354f8e939d6f1c2e.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but this is already excluded. \n\nThere is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ so we need to exclude that.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-3,1)$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(-5,0)$ contain the excluded point $x=-3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(1,5)$ and $(0,\\infty )$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=4$, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104881/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104881"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" :answer/id #uuid "9deb6886-8c97-5952-b3f7-b4bb34366e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection/05fd2b707954215b6e31003f3b5719ea407ae62ffb7fc927134bd7a3648b1020" :answer/id #uuid "fa5f3ae4-d5d9-5ff1-bfef-e19423239c5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection/88ae715561ee5b2df418683d8a7e4063045291171e7f92f28dbf20f0885b337e" :answer/id #uuid "48452b01-a88b-52cf-9e03-fd0651dc80c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-4,-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" :answer/id #uuid "a5f23e81-eb2d-5888-b487-2e61c666507e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection/d4d9d0c58dac6fecb6615f55a16d9d3e84ce77e12a45bac652d7b13344a08019" :answer/id #uuid "8dc9349d-be7d-5831-8f88-9899991392d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbb4ba81-a5d6-5c96-93a4-2eaf749f545c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48234/selection/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" :answer-field/choices ["q-48234/selection/3eaf4456135b03d48305ee199e5955cbf4f827eef281a042416764c926abc1aa" "q-48234/selection/05fd2b707954215b6e31003f3b5719ea407ae62ffb7fc927134bd7a3648b1020" "q-48234/selection/88ae715561ee5b2df418683d8a7e4063045291171e7f92f28dbf20f0885b337e" "q-48234/selection/c700f8d11f8efcb4d5adaca4943307ec7beb32a12cb40d1036833097d1b89caa" "q-48234/selection/d4d9d0c58dac6fecb6615f55a16d9d3e84ce77e12a45bac652d7b13344a08019"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48234" :question/id #uuid "8b5e7d86-6590-5293-af9e-465db1c9211b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/43786cb577705d6ae13d44e75203fef441905e9ccfc3713726f07d36c147c7b3.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity. \n\nThe function is defined on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The function is discontinuous at $x=-3$ and at $x=3,$ so we need to exclude these points..\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,3),$ and $(-3,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following: \n\n$\\,$ $(-\\infty ,-3)$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $(-4,-2)$ and $(-\\infty ,1)$ contain the excluded point $-3,$  so the function is not continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $(-2,4)$ and $(2,\\infty )$ contain the excluded point $3,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48234/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48234"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection/ab695dda7df23080d617a7397fc20ca6606d27d5f6420532cb0a8cb8416350a7" :answer/id #uuid "6aa4bbc0-6b5a-536d-bcf9-3b14c9205e33" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/deae562b9b3cbbbf07c601c0560084686a2c14453a20dd7148846e2f7d9f623f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection/d4849f06836d012cec96a79ffaae5f60f513454ae868e1287197eaed2c7e6ae9" :answer/id #uuid "269d011a-b9b7-5e56-ba7c-3698df605510" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection/6705ac26fbba8f0998899a2eb5536b85d4f2293abf1ec9190dd1c3e8114fa5bd" :answer/id #uuid "866288ea-10f1-5135-a17e-c9b0f2a12899" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04b4ed554ab43ef25caafe143aa2d4cdf280202f8a3995b10bb19e42f8ec5496.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection/4eb40f2f2dbe7edb79b67e9e9da09dac81cbb7924c599a63065c621cccbc0c7f" :answer/id #uuid "e5e05ade-3560-5bcd-bc76-4a1da47d8fd1" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/47dac620347d127e8b10c9678f09918f7ff4ccbc27cc30e224b99af38a52540b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection/f038b4371441f89d5ad206f531b911ac4f2bd8748543b5b0350c99f345a8e39a" :answer/id #uuid "a04f609a-ee17-561e-a7db-d92f6353b22b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/cb326fe00fffc0f53f396ecca4975b7ad215d3e05821cfb9b1a365657e2ba97f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d21171-d578-57ea-82cc-70f7335019a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104877/selection/d4849f06836d012cec96a79ffaae5f60f513454ae868e1287197eaed2c7e6ae9" :answer-field/choices ["q-104877/selection/ab695dda7df23080d617a7397fc20ca6606d27d5f6420532cb0a8cb8416350a7" "q-104877/selection/d4849f06836d012cec96a79ffaae5f60f513454ae868e1287197eaed2c7e6ae9" "q-104877/selection/6705ac26fbba8f0998899a2eb5536b85d4f2293abf1ec9190dd1c3e8114fa5bd" "q-104877/selection/4eb40f2f2dbe7edb79b67e9e9da09dac81cbb7924c599a63065c621cccbc0c7f" "q-104877/selection/f038b4371441f89d5ad206f531b911ac4f2bd8748543b5b0350c99f345a8e39a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104877" :question/id #uuid "1de2feaa-ecdf-5b6a-9c7c-51389b44cfed" :question/math-academy-id "q-104877" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-4,1)?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/441277a2f5c960a02d84707d21746da558e136168dc951a931f5354abf4a283e.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $g(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-4,1).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1),$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-4,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104877/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104877"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection/cbf73d33747e7378dc321a3a36a200c81edd3097e93e1a1f09c97cdfaea27c83" :answer/id #uuid "8a6b7ef9-b0ca-5c3a-8ba2-6d2a9427eb41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection/a290d61cb5e5f49798628bfb3a58f6e8586ecbcde8040ff848918986d06bc0d2" :answer/id #uuid "755741a2-1242-5d61-9b50-71d716f92b1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection/94aa507eeceffcdf0395127f3b0d8eb69718e22da6834ec0a95c67cf83eb0344" :answer/id #uuid "5b35e066-f2c2-5d40-9c3d-2b43d81f330c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,5)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection/7209a43b6c2b5cd8e9c292ea9df705bd6ad91c4f5f412791404f866b94343db5" :answer/id #uuid "e4c1981c-b4c9-5bf6-9d41-54b028b9f1a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-5,-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection/b51b20738164095cc9e115a6929df5358f5242e9eba0a5a7e61c6fe734297d94" :answer/id #uuid "4b167fde-e3e6-569e-be6a-3dd0b39aa4e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-3,10)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "479fb131-cd6d-55ca-9993-e052da3555a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104882/selection/7209a43b6c2b5cd8e9c292ea9df705bd6ad91c4f5f412791404f866b94343db5" :answer-field/choices ["q-104882/selection/cbf73d33747e7378dc321a3a36a200c81edd3097e93e1a1f09c97cdfaea27c83" "q-104882/selection/a290d61cb5e5f49798628bfb3a58f6e8586ecbcde8040ff848918986d06bc0d2" "q-104882/selection/94aa507eeceffcdf0395127f3b0d8eb69718e22da6834ec0a95c67cf83eb0344" "q-104882/selection/7209a43b6c2b5cd8e9c292ea9df705bd6ad91c4f5f412791404f866b94343db5" "q-104882/selection/b51b20738164095cc9e115a6929df5358f5242e9eba0a5a7e61c6fe734297d94"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104882" :question/id #uuid "db8e7347-ce26-542d-a0dc-df7db098b3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104882" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d068feef885b3bb1ca3770a2d2648e9953f3ca9e1823111c507934b39ad71a76.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous? You may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." :question/worked-solution "To determine the intervals of continuity of a function, we take the domain of the function and exclude any points of discontinuity.\n\nThe function is defined on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$ There is a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but this is already excluded.\n\nTherefore, the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(-1,\\infty ).$  It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $(-5,-1)$ \n\nNotice that the intervals $(-3,\\infty ),$  $(-3,5),$  $(-\\infty ,0)$  and $(-3,10)$ contain the excluded point $x=-1,$ so the function is not continuous on any of these  these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104882/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e806aac-62fc-5c53-a15e-a2ce175dad25"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104882"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection/c61aa00e14c0b5fb780f7f2a94a28691aa7109c373f4eff2435732e317255fec" :answer/id #uuid "8500702a-318c-50a4-a11a-499484196874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\pi ,2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection/de06c074562ba9bcf43d6fc8a5bad1d9599adf0df9394551fe9e51a8044dbbfb" :answer/id #uuid "b82aa7b8-9a7e-5161-9850-cc5ead98b512" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,2\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" :answer/id #uuid "44214d1a-a80f-5beb-8146-5b532d5e09e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection/5b50bf7701267a9002491b73c8a5b406d4ac20e8b37abfe852949ced34bbdba0" :answer/id #uuid "0af0f37f-ecbb-5456-99f5-b08a6639ff67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-5,0]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection/ed6c4ca02933e6d281736e8688c2347dcec20a68fcab4015c22007e7419baa9e" :answer/id #uuid "522882f7-67bb-5666-9bb8-f3442f5d995c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3df0306-cbbc-59ef-8a6b-81fa5cf9eabf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76130/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" :answer-field/choices ["q-76130/selection/c61aa00e14c0b5fb780f7f2a94a28691aa7109c373f4eff2435732e317255fec" "q-76130/selection/de06c074562ba9bcf43d6fc8a5bad1d9599adf0df9394551fe9e51a8044dbbfb" "q-76130/selection/96a865d4bb28ed6e96fc940867a37e6e9671c39a2eb0ac7f0dbfea5e98e4119e" "q-76130/selection/5b50bf7701267a9002491b73c8a5b406d4ac20e8b37abfe852949ced34bbdba0" "q-76130/selection/ed6c4ca02933e6d281736e8688c2347dcec20a68fcab4015c22007e7419baa9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76130" :question/id #uuid "44e5b666-ac66-5dd8-ae44-6b6ed7115689" :question/math-academy-id "q-76130" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5d90e9eb29955babde9d3af1ccd690b9d6b8fae6c07ad14da3867318b8487d90.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals  $(-5,0),$ and  $(0,2\\pi ).$ \n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=0$ and at $x=-5.$  \n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-5,0)$  and  $[0,2\\pi ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[0,\\pi ]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,2\\pi ]$ and $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$ contain the point $x=2\\pi ,$ but the function is not defined at this point, so it can't be continuous on these intervals.\n\n- $[-5,0]$ and $[-2,\\pi ]$  contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48293"] :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection/3b640a9ea7527601da446b0e3350e9d81527d7121a6e6d9395bc3e527cdd6823" :answer/id #uuid "55d8aebb-159a-5a5f-b20a-d1e075279cb7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e032276bfe03fe9a8e6df08e361c0a27e556e7901fd3560e5ee649220ba47aee.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection/a1614ca0d3369c136d90cf30a65dd5cc45f2e6cc49d4b04c8e4b5cc230810dbd" :answer/id #uuid "1e0d1b68-398e-5d97-b82e-0b7281edea75" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection/058eb66b6cd4de61ea6d7b9bd8eba429ab716376af8037bb3386a522165c4f09" :answer/id #uuid "6f5fb461-0212-52ce-80a1-4c1ca89b5e9a" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/2c2038b962892960e2d198038f46c844540dc70f9341487a1fa3cc85e38584db.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection/a7a7bd7ec80b2d260c4902034dcd50bcddac8ca21b1c75ef726c0d894561eb85" :answer/id #uuid "180267af-9e62-50ff-95b2-5e21665fae69" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/05c57e680f84093ea3740f206db05b239eb50ea0336b86b9c589d93497d90d9c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection/4aff26f33a40fe5d94ac356e8cec0dd05ff58bb4287e4b692840bf521b493481" :answer/id #uuid "83e54f39-8d89-5f7f-a87e-6702d0363ef0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/6abe338528df6cf89c3738319f4d8be0b87eee3dfbace1667942fc81ebeb5c4a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "69f53737-9fed-57e1-991e-c634e786aa01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76154/selection/a1614ca0d3369c136d90cf30a65dd5cc45f2e6cc49d4b04c8e4b5cc230810dbd" :answer-field/choices ["q-76154/selection/3b640a9ea7527601da446b0e3350e9d81527d7121a6e6d9395bc3e527cdd6823" "q-76154/selection/a1614ca0d3369c136d90cf30a65dd5cc45f2e6cc49d4b04c8e4b5cc230810dbd" "q-76154/selection/058eb66b6cd4de61ea6d7b9bd8eba429ab716376af8037bb3386a522165c4f09" "q-76154/selection/a7a7bd7ec80b2d260c4902034dcd50bcddac8ca21b1c75ef726c0d894561eb85" "q-76154/selection/4aff26f33a40fe5d94ac356e8cec0dd05ff58bb4287e4b692840bf521b493481"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76154" :question/id #uuid "914562d3-5f5a-58ee-b3fa-4cdc5878b28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76154" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/8bfb5e934d6ce86fc478c5ff200db616f65c95956030ccfb247e526551ee8a37.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2),$ and $(2,6).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,\\sqrt{3}),$ $(-\\sqrt{3},2],$ and $(2,6).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\sqrt{3},2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $q(x)$ is not left-continuous at $x=2,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at some point in the interval $(0,2).$  So it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-76154/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76154"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection/60785f7508fb42fe01a7ec0ebf13de116c8994c4a41510d62796befd6fd68310" :answer/id #uuid "be3e555e-576d-5656-8fe0-8fe35b2374df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection/36988ded9881cd64242c2872ed3b1061a6d84bd96627ed53c1781667f3f2f782" :answer/id #uuid "da5e02fa-5e1a-5666-a0fd-38689a0be1fe" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/f0f65307224a5fea45530fd542be70d91039bc55aa2cd8b9f17537874c5170ae.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection/0c0c074a2f5d80cc2459f501467effc40f3710bc4597e43790997f7f53c317d7" :answer/id #uuid "37fd436e-939c-593f-bb98-76d1c9d8da32" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/f13573a5bfe5661e1b9853ea559c88f48a887b3fa0618605b7f3a4c933645af3.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection/e068326b1d418d0f9b91c382877ccb97c60a7dd2f001348eadd48f0ab8a924e7" :answer/id #uuid "b7cebba2-f0e3-5dc2-b80c-8fa9ced43e10" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/5b2ca56f8c5c41015e6b0dc42311892becbb9f99233f5f9ddee951b993af95d4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection/3decf470ffa53b326e5e4473f8cf99326e052b47b3441f71171983323269e1ed" :answer/id #uuid "f85e60be-847a-5453-91c1-0e9416e62231" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/d5cfd29b266bee94ed812edb71ac9804e385c9523341816be98ac278c088ef3b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2fb4569-b862-5f80-b0a6-961887bc3cde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105181/selection/60785f7508fb42fe01a7ec0ebf13de116c8994c4a41510d62796befd6fd68310" :answer-field/choices ["q-105181/selection/60785f7508fb42fe01a7ec0ebf13de116c8994c4a41510d62796befd6fd68310" "q-105181/selection/36988ded9881cd64242c2872ed3b1061a6d84bd96627ed53c1781667f3f2f782" "q-105181/selection/0c0c074a2f5d80cc2459f501467effc40f3710bc4597e43790997f7f53c317d7" "q-105181/selection/e068326b1d418d0f9b91c382877ccb97c60a7dd2f001348eadd48f0ab8a924e7" "q-105181/selection/3decf470ffa53b326e5e4473f8cf99326e052b47b3441f71171983323269e1ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105181" :question/id #uuid "f10cb2f1-287e-54d9-a68e-50d79d0f8000" :question/math-academy-id "q-105181" :question/problem "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[1,4]?$" :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/03d8fa6c421deebb49b3746425f799a2eea7fc22f07175bb1cf80d270f896555.png)\n\n  \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $(1,5).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $[1,5).$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,1)$ and $[1,5).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[1,4]$ because $[1,4]$ is contained inside the interval $[1,5).$\n\nNotice that\n\n- $q(x)$ is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=3.$ So it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not defined at $x=4,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[1,4].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105181/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105181"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" :answer/id #uuid "26b4a72e-cf61-5d7c-9875-89713655e535" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection/c644baef54fa39bbeb49c6fc21ca4e87185f9ba25118c09aee6d6b145c957207" :answer/id #uuid "9667c274-b2ae-537f-8e44-c348998ff678" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[2,4]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection/4249ad148010e30d1550e1be789ff64a0d1d9e4c4777bb4a555e45f88863a7d9" :answer/id #uuid "fb17ef71-f1dc-5542-9d9f-b16910823fc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[0,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection/866921345ce252a704324852871bd740bbf7ef21be4f376e5ba947bff13e58a4" :answer/id #uuid "c4a09cba-4049-5743-ba0e-55de2726f6d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection/71372b0b9c1170edcc94b034ea0455d4f9d2dcc0f68d8b3661d9630669c3a7bc" :answer/id #uuid "0370ea43-5661-59db-b258-49a1d358b7ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[2,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6485ab-e4a6-57b3-ac2c-020209b5772a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-76124/selection/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" :answer-field/choices ["q-76124/selection/36504b8a59301b3a74959b47de0f45427b71e308359f3c738bbfe095208cc6f1" "q-76124/selection/c644baef54fa39bbeb49c6fc21ca4e87185f9ba25118c09aee6d6b145c957207" "q-76124/selection/4249ad148010e30d1550e1be789ff64a0d1d9e4c4777bb4a555e45f88863a7d9" "q-76124/selection/866921345ce252a704324852871bd740bbf7ef21be4f376e5ba947bff13e58a4" "q-76124/selection/71372b0b9c1170edcc94b034ea0455d4f9d2dcc0f68d8b3661d9630669c3a7bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-76124" :question/id #uuid "54f43dcc-abdb-5395-9797-01d9daa06906" :question/math-academy-id "q-76124" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/04dcf34ee71d47a340cf46334ba08ae2c010bf9c284fc79188ff13ff5d42b243.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" :question/worked-solution "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-2,1),$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-2$ and left-continuous at $x=1.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-2,1].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-2,1]$ $(1,3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-2,1]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[2,3]$ and $[2,4]$ contain the point $x=3$ at which the function is not defined, so the function is not continuous on these intervals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-76124/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36524faf-ac61-5756-89e3-c83a947ac61b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-76124"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-105402/selection/51c44db3bf62f6039de959996fa3e74c28366d1278b15f76439bc64ffc3786ba" :answer/id #uuid "f712d8b7-3811-5270-bf4e-bbac6f54a846" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/380df9811d08cf8f60f479461de0f7b1eb71e93342e69a83682e18acd6e0b027.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105402/selection/c6ef412dab2689c85a7ec8271356370b5042a5c967b8e8058da478d70cda9bdd" :answer/id #uuid "88f0187a-3940-5076-acb9-027ccb3faab7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/0121e04331b57e6b44e30c42bab437e897dce55be8af4a3819a81a8520c6a318.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105402/selection/143f90f71d224c04f24d9af20ad830f954c3d86da76bfe1512eeed750de0aaa2" :answer/id #uuid "5c8f57b5-b857-5443-9c53-45b3aa433381" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/fb960ae354431f2da96637e4af9b67f1b05a19a8efef7a29c87fcbfd763d90bf.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105402/selection/3c9967a25542810d8a81c93044a3f763bc32028fa51aa2594b47d70fa78de74b" :answer/id #uuid "d572e238-9efe-59ce-aac2-42282576f8d8" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answ